      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-sr-affine-minkowski-model",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "An **oriented time-oriented affine Minkowski space** is a quadruple\n$(A,V,\\eta,C^+)$ with the following data. In this notation $V$ includes its\nspecified orientation; forgetting that orientation gives only the underlying\nreal vector space, not the full datum $V$ of the model.\n\n1. A nonempty affine space $A$ over a $4$-dimensional real vector space $V$,\n   with action $(p,v)\\mapsto p+v$ and displacement $q-p\\in V$\n   ([[def-affine-space-and-displacement]]). In particular $V$ is a real vector\n   space of dimension $4$.\n2. A Lorentz form $\\eta$ on $V$, i.e. a nondegenerate symmetric bilinear form of\n   inertia $(3,1,0)$ ([[def-lorentz-form-and-inertia]]). Its associated quadratic\n   form is $q(v)=\\eta(v,v)$.\n3. An **orientation** of $V$: a positive ray of the determinant line\n   $\\Lambda^4V$, fixed once and for all\n   ([[def-determinant-line-orientation-of-a-finite-dimensional-real-vector-space]]).\n4. A **time orientation** $C^+$ of $(V,\\eta)$: one of the two connected\n   components of the timelike set $T=\\{v\\in V:\\eta(v,v)<0\\}$, the other being\n   $C^-:=T\\setminus C^+$ ([[def-time-orientation-and-future-causal-sets]]).\n\nThe following terminology is attached to such a quadruple.\n\n- The elements $p$ of $A$ are the **events**; for events $p,q$ the vector\n  $q-p\\in V$ is the **displacement from $p$ to $q$**.\n- The **interval** of $p,q\\in A$ is the real number\n  $I(p,q):=\\eta(q-p,q-p)=q(q-p)$.\n- Events $p,q$ are **timelike**, **null**, **spacelike** or **causally\n  related** when the displacement $q-p$ is timelike, null, spacelike or causal\n  in the sense of [[def-causal-classification-of-lorentz-vectors]]; they are\n  **future-related** when $q-p$ is future causal and **past-related** when\n  $q-p$ is past causal in the sense of\n  [[def-time-orientation-and-future-causal-sets]].\n\n**No point of $A$ is distinguished.** For every $v\\in V$ the translation\n$\\tau_v:A\\to A$, $\\tau_v(p):=p+v$, is a bijection, since it is inverse to\n$\\tau_{-v}$: $\\tau_{-v}(\\tau_v(p))=(p+v)+(-v)=p+(v-v)=p$ and symmetrically, by\nthe affine action axioms ([[def-affine-space-and-displacement]]). Displacements\nare translation invariant, $\\tau_v(q)-\\tau_v(p)=q-p$, so every $\\tau_v$ preserves\nthe interval, the causal classification of every ordered pair, and the sets\n$C^+$, $C^-$ (which are subsets of $V$, independent of the point of $A$); it\nchanges only the labelling of points. The mathematical structure of the model is\nexactly the quadruple $(A,V,\\eta,C^+)$; no derived physical quantity (proper\ntime, four-velocity, momentum, energy, charge or any field) is part of this\ndefinition, and all later structure of the framework (frames, charts, tensor\nfields, worldlines) is built on this model.\n\n**Well-definedness.** The affine-space definition supplies the action and the\nunique displacements; the Lorentz-form definition supplies $\\eta$ and its\ninertia; the determinant-line definition supplies orientations; and the two\ncomponents of $T$ between which the time-orientation definition chooses exist by\nthe components lemma recorded in the well-definedness clause of\n[[def-time-orientation-and-future-causal-sets]], which is also what makes the\nnames future and past causal in item 4 well defined.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-time-orientation-and-future-causal-sets",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $(V,\\eta)$ be a Lorentz vector space of signature $(-,+,+,+)$\n([[def-lorentz-form-and-inertia]]), with causal terminology as in\n[[def-causal-classification-of-lorentz-vectors]], and let\n\n$$T:=\\{v\\in V:\\eta(v,v)<0\\}$$\n\nbe its **timelike set**. Components and closures refer to the topology obtained\nby using any ordered basis to identify $V$ with Euclidean $\\mathbb R^4$;\n[[lem-timelike-cone-has-two-convex-components]], step 5.1, proves that the\nresulting open sets are independent of the basis. By that lemma the\nset $T$ has exactly two connected components, and each of them is nonempty,\nopen, convex and path-connected. A **time orientation** of $(V,\\eta)$ is a\nchoice of one of these two components. Fixing such a choice, write $C^+$ for the\nchosen component and\n\n$$C^-:=T\\setminus C^+$$\n\nfor the other component; thus $T=C^+\\sqcup C^-$ is a disjoint union and\n$C^+$, $C^-$ are exactly the two components supplied by the lemma. Relative to\nthis choice a vector $v\\in V$ is\n\n- **future timelike** when $v\\in C^+$;\n- **past timelike** when $v\\in C^-$;\n- **future null** when $v\\neq 0$, $\\eta(v,v)=0$ and $v\\in\\operatorname{cl}(C^+)$;\n- **past null** when $v\\neq 0$, $\\eta(v,v)=0$ and $v\\in\\operatorname{cl}(C^-)$;\n- **future causal** when it is future timelike or future null;\n- **past causal** when it is past timelike or past null.\n\nThe sets $\\operatorname{cl}(C^+)$ and $\\operatorname{cl}(C^-)$ are the\n**future closed cone** and the **past closed cone** of the time-oriented space\n$(V,\\eta,C^+)$. By [[lem-timelike-cone-has-two-convex-components]] each of them\nis a convex cone, their intersection is $\\{0\\}$, and a vector is causal exactly\nwhen it is future causal or past causal, in which case exactly one of these two\nalternatives holds: no nonzero vector is both future causal and past causal.\nThe timelike vectors are exactly the points of $T=C^+\\cup C^-$, and\n$0\\notin T$ because $\\eta(0,0)=0$; since a null vector is nonzero with\n$\\eta(v,v)=0$, no vector is both timelike and null, so the classes above are\nmutually exclusive where the definitions say they are: no vector is both future\nand past causal, and no timelike vector is null.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-inertial-frame-and-inertial-chart",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $(A,V,\\eta,C^+)$ be an oriented time-oriented affine Minkowski space\n([[def-sr-affine-minkowski-model]]), with orientation fixed as a positive ray of\nthe determinant line $\\Lambda^4V$\n([[def-determinant-line-orientation-of-a-finite-dimensional-real-vector-space]])\nand with the corresponding class of positively oriented ordered bases of $V$\n([[def-orientation-of-a-finite-dimensional-real-vector-space]]); by\n[[prop-orientations-and-positive-basis-classes-agree-in-positive-dimension]] the\npositive ray selects exactly one such class for $\\dim V=4>0$.\n\nAn **inertial frame** of the model is a tuple $(o;e_0,e_1,e_2,e_3)$ consisting\nof an event $o\\in A$ and an ordered basis $(e_0,e_1,e_2,e_3)$ of $V$ that is\n\n1. **$\\eta$-orthonormal**: $\\eta(e_a,e_b)=\\operatorname{diag}(-1,1,1,1)_{ab}$ for all $a,b$ ([[lem-lorentz-form-has-orthonormal-basis]]);\n2. **future**: $e_0\\in C^+$, i.e. $e_0$ is future timelike; and\n3. **positively oriented**: the ordered basis lies in the positive-basis class\n   selected by the fixed orientation.\n\nThe event $o$ is the **origin** of the frame and $e_0,e_1,e_2,e_3$ are its\n**basis vectors**.\n\n**Inertial charts.** For an inertial frame $(o;e_0,e_1,e_2,e_3)$, define\n\n$$E:\\mathbb R^4\\longrightarrow V,\\qquad E(u):=\\sum_{a=0}^3u^ae_a .$$\n\nThen $E$ is a linear isomorphism ([[def-linear-isomorphism-and-invertible-linear-map]]):\nit is linear by construction, and it is bijective because\n$(e_0,e_1,e_2,e_3)$ is an ordered basis, every $v\\in V$ having exactly one\ncoordinate list $u$ with $v=\\sum_au^ae_a$\n([[thm-unique-coordinates-with-respect-to-an-ordered-basis]]). The **inertial\nchart** of the frame is the map\n\n$$x:A\\longrightarrow\\mathbb R^4,\\qquad x(p):=E^{-1}(p-o),$$\n\nand its values $x^a(p)$ are the **inertial coordinates of the event $p$** in the\nframe. Since $E$ and $v\\mapsto p+v$ are bijections, $x$ is a bijection\n$A\\to\\mathbb R^4$; and $x(p)=0$ exactly when $p-o=0_V$, that is, exactly when\n$p=o$.\n\n**Transition law.** Let $(o';e'_0,e'_1,e'_2,e'_3)$ be another inertial frame\nwith chart $x'$, and let $M=(M^b_a)$ be the change-of-basis matrix defined by\n$e'_a=\\sum_bM^b_ae_b$, a $4\\times4$ real matrix that is invertible because both\nfamilies are ordered bases. Then $E'(u)=\\sum_au^ae'_a=E(Mu)$ for all\n$u\\in\\mathbb R^4$, for the matrix-vector product $Mu$ in the coordinates of the\nfirst frame, because $\\sum_au^a\\sum_bM^b_ae_b=\\sum_b(\\sum_aM^b_au^a)e_b$; hence\n$E'=E\\circ M$ and $E'^{-1}=M^{-1}\\circ E^{-1}$, so for every event $p$,\n\n$$x'(p)=M^{-1}\\bigl(x(p)-x(o')\\bigr)=M^{-1}x(p)-M^{-1}x(o') .$$\n\nIn particular the transition between two inertial charts is an affine map of\n$\\mathbb R^4$ with invertible linear part $M^{-1}$ and constant part\n$-M^{-1}x(o')$, and it is a bijection of $\\mathbb R^4$ with inverse\n$u\\mapsto Mu+x(o')$, which is the transition in the reverse direction.\n\n**The inertial atlas.** The **inertial atlas** of the model is the family of\nall inertial frames together with their inertial charts. Its charts are\navailable at every event by [[lem-future-oriented-inertial-frames-exist]], which\nsupplies for every $o\\in A$ an inertial frame with origin $o$; and any two of\nits charts are related by the affine transition displayed above.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "def-directional-and-partial-derivatives",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $U\\subseteq\\mathbb R^m$, $a\\in U$, $v\\in\\mathbb R^m$, and $f:U\\to\\mathbb R^n$. If the line map $t\\mapsto f(a+tv)$ is defined near $0$, its derivative at $0$ is the **directional derivative**\n\n$$D_vf(a):=\\lim_{t\\to0}\\frac{f(a+tv)-f(a)}{t}.$$\n\nFor a standard basis vector $e_j$ ([[lem-standard-basis-of-f-n]]), $D_{e_j}f(a)$ is the **$j$th partial derivative**, written $\\partial_jf(a)$. These are ordinary vector-valued one-variable derivatives in the sense of [[def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral]].",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "thm-algebra-of-derivatives",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $A \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}$, let $c \\in A$ be a limit point of $A$\n([[def-limit-point-r]]), let $f, g : A \\to \\mathbb{R}$ be differentiable at $c$\n([[def-derivative]]) and let $\\alpha \\in \\mathbb{R}$. Then:\n\n1. $f + g$ is differentiable at $c$ and $(f+g)'(c) = f'(c) + g'(c)$;\n2. $\\alpha f$ is differentiable at $c$ and $(\\alpha f)'(c) = \\alpha f'(c)$;\n3. $fg$ is differentiable at $c$ and $(fg)'(c) = f'(c)g(c) + f(c)g'(c)$;\n4. if $g(c) \\ne 0$ then, writing $A_0 := \\{\\, x \\in A : g(x) \\ne 0 \\,\\}$, the\n   point $c$ lies in $A_0$ and is a limit point of $A_0$, the quotient\n   $(f/g)|_{A_0} : A_0 \\to \\mathbb{R}$, $x \\mapsto f(x)/g(x)$, is differentiable\n   at $c$ as a function on $A_0$, and\n   $$\\bigl((f/g)|_{A_0}\\bigr)'(c) \\;=\\; \\frac{f'(c)\\,g(c) - f(c)\\,g'(c)}{g(c)^{2}} .$$\n\nEach claim asserts two things: that the derivative on the left exists, and that\nit has the stated value. Both are proved.\n\n**Why claim 4 is stated on $A_0$.** The function $f/g$ is not defined where $g$\nvanishes, and $g$ may vanish at points of $A$ far from $c$; restricting to $A_0$\nis forced. That the restriction still has $c$ as a limit point, so that a\nderivative there means anything at all, is not free either, and it is the last\nclaim of [[lem-sign-preservation-near-a-limit]] applied to $g$. The hypothesis\nis $g(c) \\ne 0$, not \"$g$ vanishes nowhere\".\n\n**Everything is proved through\n[[thm-caratheodory-characterisation]].** No difference quotient is estimated and\nno limit theorem beyond continuity is used, so no choice principle is spent.\nThe four identities are four algebraic rearrangements of an increment, each\nfollowed by a reading of [[thm-algebra-of-continuous-functions]].",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "thm-chain-rule",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $A, B \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}$, let $g : A \\to \\mathbb{R}$ with\n$g[A] \\subseteq B$ and let $f : B \\to \\mathbb{R}$, so that the composite\n$f \\circ g : A \\to \\mathbb{R}$ is defined. Let $c \\in A$ be a limit point of\n$A$ ([[def-limit-point-r]]) at which $g$ is differentiable\n([[def-derivative]]), put $b := g(c)$, and suppose $b$ is a limit point of $B$\nat which $f$ is differentiable. Then $f \\circ g$ is differentiable at $c$ and\n\n$$(f \\circ g)'(c) \\;=\\; f'\\bigl(g(c)\\bigr)\\,g'(c) .$$\n\n**Both limit-point hypotheses are needed, and neither is automatic.** That $c$\nis a limit point of $A$ is what makes $g'(c)$ and $(f \\circ g)'(c)$ defined\nsymbols; that $b = g(c)$ is a limit point of $B$ is what makes $f'(b)$ one.\nNothing forces the second: $g$ may be differentiable at $c$ and send $c$ to an\nisolated point of $B$, and there $f'(b)$ is not defined and the formula asserts\nnothing.\n\n**No case analysis appears anywhere.** The naive difference-quotient proof\nwrites $\\frac{f(g(x)) - f(g(c))}{g(x) - g(c)} \\cdot \\frac{g(x) - g(c)}{x - c}$\nand then has to say what happens where $g(x) = g(c)$, which may occur at points\narbitrarily close to $c$. Carathéodory's factorisation never divides by the\ninner increment, so the difficulty does not arise.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "thm-picard-lindelof-local-existence-and-uniqueness",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $D\\subseteq\\mathbb R\\times\\mathbb R^n$ be open, let $F:D\\to\\mathbb R^n$ be continuous and locally Lipschitz in the state variable, and let $(t_0,x_0)\\in D$. Then a unique local solution of the IVP exists on some interval around the initial time. More quantitatively, if $h,r>0$ make\n\n$$[t_0-h,t_0+h]\\times\\overline B(x_0,r)\\subseteq D,$$\n\nif $\\|F\\|_2\\le M$ and $F$ has state-Lipschitz constant $L$ on this cylinder, and if $hM\\le r$ and $Lh<1$, then there is exactly one solution on $[t_0-h,t_0+h]$ through $(t_0,x_0)$ whose graph lies in the cylinder. Any two solutions through the same initial data agree on every common subinterval containing $t_0$.\n\nIn particular, a unique local solution exists on an interval around the initial time.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "thm-heine-borel-rn",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $n \\in \\mathbb{N}$ with $n \\ge 1$, let $\\mathbb{R}^n$ be the set of functions\n$n \\to \\mathbb{R}$ and let $d_2$ be the Euclidean metric on it\n([[lem-metrics-on-rn]]). Then:\n\n1. **Closed boxes are compact.** For reals $a_k \\le b_k$ $(k < n)$ the box\n   $Q = \\{\\, x \\in \\mathbb{R}^n : a_k \\le x_k \\le b_k \\text{ for every } k < n \\,\\}$\n   is a compact subset of $(\\mathbb{R}^n, d_2)$ ([[def-metric-compactness]]).\n2. **Heine-Borel.** A subset $K \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}^n$ is a compact subset of\n   $(\\mathbb{R}^n, d_2)$ if and only if $K$ is closed in $\\mathbb{R}^n$\n   ([[def-metric-topology]]) and bounded\n   ([[def-metric-bounded-diameter]]).\n3. **The real line.** A subset $K \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}$ is a compact subset of\n   $(\\mathbb{R}, d_{\\mathbb{R}})$, the usual metric $d_{\\mathbb{R}}(x,y) = |x-y|$\n   ([[lem-real-line-is-a-metric-space]]), if and only if $K$ is closed in\n   $\\mathbb{R}$ and bounded.\n\n**No choice principle is used.** The bisection below halves one coordinate at a\ntime and takes the **left** half whenever the left half still fails to be finitely\ncovered, the right half otherwise: a rule with two outcomes, decided by a\nproperty of the box, not a selection. That is the whole reason the theorem is\navailable in ZF, while the general \"complete and totally bounded implies compact\"\n([[thm-complete-and-totally-bounded-implies-compact]]) is not.\n\nThe hypothesis $n \\ge 1$ is inherited from [[lem-metrics-on-rn]], which defines\n$\\mathbb{R}^n$ and its metrics only there; the last remark below records what\nhappens at $n = 0$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "thm-extreme-value-metric",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $(X,d)$ be a nonempty compact metric space ([[def-metric-compactness]],\n[[def-metric-space]]) and let $f : X \\to \\mathbb{R}$ be continuous\n([[def-metric-continuity]]), $\\mathbb{R}$ carrying its usual metric\n$d_{\\mathbb{R}}(s,t) = |s-t|$ ([[lem-real-line-is-a-metric-space]]). Then the\nimage $f[X]$ is bounded above and below ([[def-bounded-set]]), and it has a\nmaximum and a minimum ([[def-max-min]]): there are points\n$x_{\\max}, x_{\\min} \\in X$ with\n\n$$f(x_{\\min}) \\;\\le\\; f(x) \\;\\le\\; f(x_{\\max}) \\qquad \\text{for every } x \\in X,$$\n\nand then $f(x_{\\max}) = \\sup f[X]$ and $f(x_{\\min}) = \\inf f[X]$\n([[def-complete-ordered-field]], [[def-infimum]]).\n\nNonemptiness of $X$ is a hypothesis and not an oversight: for $X = \\emptyset$ the\nimage is empty and has neither a supremum nor a maximum. No choice principle is\nused.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F7",
          "source": "cor-mean-value-theorem",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $a, b \\in \\mathbb{R}$ with $a < b$ and let $f : [a,b] \\to \\mathbb{R}$ be\ncontinuous on $[a,b]$ ([[def-continuity-real]], [[def-interval]]) and\ndifferentiable at every point of $(a,b)$ as a function on $[a,b]$\n([[def-derivative]]). Then there is $c \\in (a,b)$ with\n\n$$f(b) - f(a) \\;=\\; f'(c)\\,(b - a) .$$\n\nEquivalently, since $b - a \\ne 0$, there is $c \\in (a,b)$ at which\n$f'(c) = \\bigl(f(b)-f(a)\\bigr)/(b-a)$: the derivative somewhere inside equals\nthe average rate of change across the whole interval.\n\n**Continuity on the closed interval cannot be dropped.** Differentiability at\nevery point of $(a,b)$ alone does not suffice: a function on $[0,1]$,\ndifferentiable at every point of $(0,1)$ with derivative constantly $1$, for\nwhich no $c$ works, is exhibited later on this page as a false statement, and\nthe companion page works the same witness out in full.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F8",
          "source": "thm-intermediate-value",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $a, b \\in \\mathbb{R}$ with $a \\le b$, let $f : [a,b] \\to \\mathbb{R}$ be\ncontinuous on the closed bounded interval $[a,b]$ ([[def-interval]],\n[[def-continuity-real]]), and let $y \\in \\mathbb{R}$ satisfy\n\n$$f(a) \\le y \\le f(b) \\qquad \\text{or} \\qquad f(b) \\le y \\le f(a) .$$\n\nThen there is $c \\in [a,b]$ with $f(c) = y$.\n\n**The construction is canonical, so no choice principle is used.** The bisection\nbelow tests the left half first and takes the right half only when the left one\nhas been ruled out, so the passage from one interval to the next is a *function*\nof that interval, and [[thm-recursion]] applies with nothing selected. This is\nthe same discipline the library uses wherever a sequence is built one term at a\ntime.\n\n**Completeness of $\\mathbb{R}$ is what does the work.** The bisection produces a\nnested sequence of closed bounded intervals whose lengths tend to $0$, and it is\n[[thm-nested-interval-property]] — hence the least-upper-bound property — that\nsupplies the point. Over an ordered field that is not complete the theorem\nfails; the witness for that, stated for an arbitrary ordered field and worked\nover $\\mathbb{Q}$, is\n[[cex-evt-and-ivt-fail-over-a-non-complete-field]], which states its own\n$\\varepsilon$-$\\delta$ continuity inline and is not an instance of this page's\ndefinition.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "Antisymmetry gives $\\eta(U,f_0)=\\alpha U^aF_{ab}U^b=0$. On the unit shell, $U_a\\Pi_U{}^a{}_b=U_b+(\\eta(U,U)/c^2)U_b=0$, so $\\eta(U,a_{\\rm LL})=0$. The coordinate right-hand side $(U,a_{\\rm LL}(x,U))$ is $C^1$ on the open state domain $O\\times V$: $F$ is $C^2$, its partials [F3] are $C^1$, and the remaining factors are polynomial in $U$, using [F4]. In a closed coordinate box around any state, its continuous first derivatives are bounded by [F6]; applying [F7] componentwise along line segments proves the field locally Lipschitz. Thus [F5] gives a unique local full-state solution through $(x_0,U_0)$.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "A1",
            "F3",
            "F4",
            "F5",
            "F6",
            "F7",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-1-2",
          "claim": "For arbitrary $U$, antisymmetry gives $\\eta(U,f_0)=0$ and the projector formula gives $\\eta(U,\\Pi_U B)=(1+\\eta(U,U)/c^2)\\eta(U,B)$. Along the full solution put $h(\\tau)=\\eta(U,U)+c^2$; then\n\n$$\nh'(\\tau)=2\\eta(U,a_{\\rm LL})=\\frac{2\\tau_e}{c^2}\\eta(U,B)h(\\tau).\n$$\n\nThe coefficient is continuous. The zero function solves this scalar linear IVP with $h(0)=0$, and the scalar vector field is locally Lipschitz in $h$ because it is linear in that variable, so uniqueness [F5] gives $h=0$ on the full common interval. Thus the proper-time normalization $\\eta(U,U)=-c^2$ is preserved.",
          "step": "1.2",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "A1",
            "F4",
            "F5",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "In the inertial frame of [F2], $e_0$ has coordinates $(1,0,0,0)$. For any future timelike $W$, convexity of $C^+$ makes $(1-s)e_0+sW$ timelike for $0\\le s\\le1$. A timelike vector cannot have zeroth component zero, since then its squared norm would be the nonnegative spatial norm square. If $W^0\\le0$, the continuous zeroth component along this segment takes the value zero by [F8], a contradiction. Thus every future timelike vector has $W^0>0$. Along the solution, the preserved shell makes $U$ timelike; if $U^0$ became nonpositive, [F8] on the closed interval between that time and $0$ would force a zero component, again impossible. Since the initial vector is future, $U$ remains in $C^+$ throughout the local interval. No continuation beyond this interval is asserted.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F8",
            "step 1.2",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
