    "rem-rpm-self-force-status": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-rpm-body-moments",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Use the charged/spinning body-model conventions of the now-authored batch-24 carrier [[def-rpm-charge-spin-model]]: a smooth time-oriented Lorentzian four-manifold of signature $(-+++)$, coordinates $x^0=ct$ in metres, $c>0$, and a $C^2$ future timelike representative curve $z:I\\to M$ parametrized by proper time $\\tau$ in seconds, with $U=dz/d\\tau$ and $g(U,U)=-c^2$. The actual supplier defines these quantities and the SI electromagnetic-tensor convention used here. Assume Countable Choice for the GR smooth-structure, convex-normal and parallel-transport suppliers used below.\n\n**(a) Flat isolated-body moments.** In an inertial chart of affine Minkowski space, let $T^{ab}$ be a smooth symmetric stress-energy tensor with components in $\\mathrm J\\,\\mathrm m^{-3}$ and spatially compact support on every finite time slab. At a fixed time $t$, define\n$$p^a(t):=\\frac1c\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}T^{0a}(ct,\\mathbf x)\\,d^3x,$$\nand, for a representative point $z(t)$ on that time slice, define\n$$S_z^{ab}(t):=\\frac1c\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}\\left((x-z(t))^aT^{0b}(ct,\\mathbf x)-(x-z(t))^bT^{0a}(ct,\\mathbf x)\\right)d^3x.$$\nHere $(x-z(t))^0=0$ on the chosen slice, $p$ has units $\\mathrm{kg\\,m\\,s^{-1}}$, and $S_z$ has units $\\mathrm{kg\\,m^2\\,s^{-1}}$. These are definitions of moments; no conservation is asserted by clause (a).\n\n**(b) Local curved-body moments.** Let $z\\in M$ and let $U_0$ be a convex normal neighborhood containing $z$. Let $\\Sigma\\subset U_0$ be a spacelike $C^1$ hypersurface through $z$, with future unit normal $n_x$ and induced volume element $d\\Sigma$. Let a smooth symmetric stress-energy tensor $T^{ab}$ have support on $\\Sigma$ compactly contained in $U_0$, with all displayed integrals finite. For each support point $x\\in\\Sigma$, let $v(x):=\\exp_z^{-1}(x)\\in T_zM$ be the unique joining position vector supplied by [[thm-gr-convex-normal-neighborhoods]] and [[thm-gr-local-geodesics-and-exponential-map]]. Let $H_{x\\to z}:T_xM\\to T_zM$ be parallel transport along that unique geodesic, with existence, uniqueness and metric preservation as in [[lem-gr-parallel-transport-and-geodesic-speed]]. Define $T^\\sharp(n_x)^a:=T^{ab}(x)(n_x)_b$ and\n$$p_z:=\\frac1c\\int_\\Sigma H_{x\\to z}\\!\\left(-T^\\sharp(n_x)\\right)d\\Sigma,\\qquad S_z:=\\frac1c\\int_\\Sigma v(x)\\wedge H_{x\\to z}\\!\\left(-T^\\sharp(n_x)\\right)d\\Sigma,$$\nwhere $(v\\wedge w)^{ab}:=v^aw^b-v^bw^a$ and the finite-dimensional vector and tensor integrals are taken componentwise in any basis of $T_zM$. The resulting $p_z$ and $S_z$ are a vector and an antisymmetric tensor at $z$; their units are $\\mathrm{kg\\,m\\,s^{-1}}$ and $\\mathrm{kg\\,m^2\\,s^{-1}}$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "post-rpm-lad-effective-equation",
          "source_section": "Postulate",
          "quote": "Restrict [[def-rpm-charge-spin-model]] to its flat affine Minkowski branch, with signature $(-,+,+,+)$, proper time $\\tau$ in seconds, future four-velocity $U=dx/d\\tau$ satisfying $\\eta(U,U)=-c^2$, constant primitive $m>0$ in kilograms and nonzero charge $q\\in\\mathbb R$ in coulombs. Let the prescribed external electromagnetic two-form $F$ be $C^2$ on an open neighborhood of the worldline; use the component convention $F_{0i}=-E_i/c$, $F_{ij}=\\epsilon_{ijk}B_k$ of [[def-em-electromagnetic-tensor]]. Let $a:=dU/d\\tau$ and\n\n$$\n\\alpha:=q/m,\\qquad\n\\tau_e:=\\frac{q^2}{6\\pi\\epsilon_0mc^3}>0,\\qquad\nf_0^a:=\\alpha F^a{}_bU^b,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\epsilon_0=(\\mu_0c^2)^{-1}$ follows [[def-em-si-conventions]]. Adopt the flat LAD effective equation\n\n$$\na^a=f_0^a+\\tau_e\\left(\\frac{da^a}{d\\tau}-\\frac{\\eta_{bc}a^ba^c}{c^2}U^a\\right).\n$$\n\nThe derivative is the ordinary derivative in an inertial affine chart. Its dimensions are consistent: $a$ and $f_0$ are accelerations, the parenthesized term has acceleration per second, and $\\tau_e$ is a time. This is an adopted effective model equation for sufficiently regular curves (at least $C^3$); it is not asserted to be an exact law of nature, a point-source Maxwell construction, or a globally well-posed dynamics. In particular, no existence, uniqueness, stability, or empirical claim is part of this postulate.\n\nUse of the equation as a reduced physical approximation carries the source qualification that radiation reaction be small relative to the applied Lorentz force in the particle's instantaneous rest frame (the inertial frame momentarily comoving with $U$). This postulate neither asserts that condition for arbitrary $F$ nor supplies a numerical threshold; the qualification is not used to deduce the local ODE results or the force-free counterexample.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "The flat moments and local curved moments in [F1] are definitions for smooth finite-body stress-energy data; the supplier explicitly says no slice-independence theorem or equation of motion is asserted. Thus these moment definitions alone do not specify a point-particle limit or a value of the self-field on the representative worldline.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1"
          ]
        },
